{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And on your new moons.</b> Now, the day of the new moon was a custom for Israel to be a holy day in some respect, as he testified by saying (I Samuel 20:19) ״אֲשֶׁר נִסְתַּרְתָּ שָׁם בְּיוֹם הַמַּעֲשֶׂה״, and this indicates that the day of the new moon was not among them a working day. And therefore He attributes it to Israel by saying רָאשֵׁי חָדְשֵׁיכֶם — and not so did He write regarding the festivals, for He did not write ״בְּשַׁבַּת שַׁבַּתְּכֶם״ nor ״בְּיוֹם בִּכּוּרֵיכֶם״ nor ״בְּיוֹם סֻכּוֹתֵיכֶם״. And the reason for this custom of theirs was that Israel's success in this world is indeed in some respect like the matter of the moon, which has no light of its own at all except what it receives from something other than itself. And this is that even though before the calf there was ״חֵרוּת עַל הַלֻּחוֹת״ (Avot 6:2) — freedom from subjugation to the kingdoms of the world — nevertheless after they sinned they recovered but did not continuously enjoy the crown of sovereignty like the other nations; rather, this was theirs at times, according to when the supernal light shone upon them, and without it they walked in darkness with no brightness, as happens to the moon when it does not receive the light of the sun, for ״אֵין מַזָּל לְיִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Shabbat 156a) — they have no light at all from themselves, except the light of the Blessed El when they find favor. And thus the Blessed El is called by the prophets ״אוֹר יִשְׂרָאֵל״, as He said (Isaiah 10:17) ״וְהָיָה אוֹר יִשְׂרָאֵל לְאֵשׁ״, and so He testified by saying (Psalms 27:1) ״ה׳ אוֹרִי וְיִשְׁעִי״. And when sins separated, as He said (Isaiah 59:2) ״כִּי אִם עֲוֹנוֹתֵיכֶם הָיוּ מַבְדִּילִים בֵּינֵיכֶם לְבֵין אֱלֹהֵיכֶם, וְחַטֹּאתֵיכֶם הִסְתִּירוּ פָנִים מִכֶּם מִשְּׁמוֹעַ״, they went in bewildered and pressed darkness among the nations. And since from this there resulted profanation of HaShem's name without doubt, in that it was said of them (Ezekiel 36:20) ״עַם ה׳ אֵלֶּה״, it was in some manner (Isaiah 63:9) ״בְּכָל צָרָתָם לוֹ צָר״, as they of blessed memory said: ״כִּבְיָכוֹל עָלַי וְעָלֶיךָ בָּא שׁוֹד שׁוֹדֵד״ (Gittin 58a). And since the new-moon sin-offering atones for Israel, who cause the withholding or diminution of the light — as they of blessed memory instituted by saying ״שְׂעִירֵי חַטָּאת לְכַפֵּר בַּעֲדָם, זִכָּרוֹן לְכֻלָּם יִהְיוּ, תְּשׁוּעַת נַפְשָׁם מִיַּד שׂוֹנֵא״ — and through this atonement there will be some deliverance for the profanation of the Name, therefore they said that for this reason it is said of the new-moon goat ״חַטָּאת לַה׳״, because its atonement serves to sanctify His blessed Name. And this is what they intended by saying (Chullin 60b) ״אָמַר הַקָּדוֹשׁ בָּרוּךְ הוּא הָבִיאוּ כַּפָּרָה עָלַי עַל שֶׁמִּעַטְתִּי אֶת הַיָּרֵחַ״ — meaning: bring atonement on My behalf to achieve the sanctification of My Name that is profaned among the nations because of the fact that I diminished the moon, when I exiled Israel and hid My face from them."], :book :numbers, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:41:31.363467583Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}